    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "**Yes, tentatively prioritise it for the next study.** The nominal margin is:\n\n`30 \u00b5M / 0.05 \u00b5M = 600-fold`\n\nThat is reassuring, but it is not by itself a cardiac-safety conclusion. The comparison is only valid if the hERG concentration is an unbound concentration and if the free Cmax captures relevant peak exposure. Active metabolites, assay temperature/protocol, and exposure at supratherapeutic doses remain unknown.\n\n**Test next:** run a confirmatory, exposure-driven cardiac safety package:\n\n- Repeat hERG concentration-response testing using physiologically relevant conditions and include major human metabolites.\n- Test key counter-ion channels, especially Nav1.5 and Cav1.2, to detect mixed-channel liabilities.\n- Follow with in vivo telemetry/QTc at intended and supra-therapeutic exposures, with measured parent and metabolite concentrations.\n\nA finding of QT/QTc effects, meaningful metabolite-mediated hERG inhibition, or a substantially narrower exposure margin would change the prioritisation.",
